  "sigkill_test_scope": {
    "does_not_establish": [
      "DURABILITY against power loss or kernel panic. SIGKILL destroys the process but leaves the page cache intact, so data written-but-not-fsynced still reaches disk. Only a crash of the whole machine, or a fault-injecting filesystem, distinguishes fsynced from merely written. The fsync calls are therefore reasoned, not measured.",
      "atomicity of os.replace on a non-POSIX or network filesystem. On Kaggle's or Colab's overlay/network-backed working directories, rename may not be atomic and fsync may not reach stable storage; the whole protocol rests on both.",
      "a kill INSIDE a third-party write. A SIGKILL during Keras's multi-file zip write, or inside the real firewall's own save, cannot be scheduled deterministically; those are covered by writing through our own atomic wrapper instead.",
      "anything about concurrent writers. One process per run directory is assumed and not enforced by a lock."
    ],
    "establishes": [
      "that the ORDERING of operations is correct: whatever a later process finds on disk is a state the protocol can interpret, at every named boundary",
      "that no `except`, `finally` or `atexit` path is relied upon, because SIGKILL runs none of them",
      "that a partially written file, an orphaned inflight file, a lagging log or a torn trailing line is recoverable rather than fatal",
      "that the recovered epoch's weights and optimizer state correspond to that epoch, checked by reading the committed slot's contents"
    ]
  },
